  "slug": "diabetes-driven-7f07",
  "summary": "Public transcriptomes and multi-omics layers are routinely combined with machine learning to narrow candidate biomarkers across diseases.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is candidate prioritization from transcriptomic and multi-omics data, with several items using public or cohort datasets to nominate genes, proteins, or signatures. Examples include LILRB3 as a ulcerative colitis biomarker from integrated bulk and single-cell transcriptomics with machine learning, FOS in chronic atrophic gastritis via virtual knockout, and 28 plasma proteins linked causally to esophageal adenocarcinoma by Mendelian randomization of cis-pQTLs (n=35,559). Newer elements include virtual knockout as a perturbation proxy and MR-based protein prioritization. Other items are mechanistic rather than discovery-stage, including circFOXK2 regulating MLL1 for H3K4 methylation, SUZ12 silencing NCOA4-mediated ferroptosis, and butyrate-altered DEGs in lymphoblastoid cell lines. Several are reviews or network-pharmacology studies, so causal and translational claims remain largely hypothesis-generating.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-30"
}
